# Q9. Difference Between `git pull`, `git fetch`, and `git clone`

| Command | Description | When to Use |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`git clone`** | Creates a complete local copy of a remote repository, including all files, branches, and commit history. | Use when downloading a repository for the first time. |
| **`git fetch`** | Downloads the latest commits, files, and refs from the remote repository **without** modifying your local working branch. | Use when you want to review remote changes before merging them. |
| **`git pull`** | Downloads changes from the remote repository and **automatically merges** them into your current local branch. | Use when you want to quickly update your local repository with the latest changes. |

> 💡 **Quick Formula:** `git pull` = `git fetch` + `git merge`

### Summary
* **`git clone`** → Creates a brand new local copy of a remote repository.
* **`git fetch`** → Retrieves remote updates safely without changing your local files.
* **`git pull`** → Retrieves remote updates and immediately merges them into your current branch.

